\label{thm:mirsky}
The set of primes $p$ for which $p - 1$ is squarefree has relative density
$\prod_{q} \left(1 - \frac{1}{q(q-1)}\right) = 0.3739558\dots$ (Artin's constant) within the primes.

\label{thm:coprime}
Let $x = 6k+1$ for an integer $k \ge 0$. The six pairwise gcds of the quantities $x$, $6x-1$, $2x-1$, $6x-7$ satisfy
\begin{align*}
\gcd(x, 6x{-}1) = \gcd(x, 2x{-}1) &= \gcd(6x{-}1, 2x{-}1) \\
= \gcd(6x{-}1, 6x{-}7) &= \gcd(2x{-}1, 6x{-}7) = 1,
\end{align*}
while $\gcd(x, 6x-7) = \gcd(x, 7) \in \{1, 7\}$, with value $7$ exactly when $7 \mid x$. Moreover $7 \mid x$ if and only if $7 \mid 6x - 7$, but $49$ divides at most one of $x$ and $6x-7$.

If the order is $m$, then $q^2 \mid 2^m - 1$; so it suffices to check that no $2^m - 1$ with $m \le 19$ is divisible by the square of a prime $\ge 5$. The factorizations are
\begin{align*}
&1,\ 3,\ 7,\ 3\cdot5,\ 31,\ 3^2\cdot7,\ 127,\ 3\cdot5\cdot17,\ 7\cdot73,\ 3\cdot11\cdot31,\ 23\cdot89,\ 3^2\cdot5\cdot7\cdot13,\\
&8191,\ 3\cdot43\cdot127,\ 7\cdot31\cdot151,\ 3\cdot5\cdot17\cdot257,\ 131071,\ 3^3\cdot7\cdot19\cdot73,\ 524287
\end{align*}
for $m = 1, \dots, 19$; the only prime squares occurring are powers of $3$ (at $m = 6, 12, 18$), which are excluded since $q \ge 5$. Hence the order is at least $20$ for every $q \ge 5$, and the factorization $2^{20} - 1 = 3 \cdot 5^2 \cdot 11 \cdot 31 \cdot 41$ shows that the order of $2$ modulo $25$ is exactly $20$, while for $q > 5$ the order exceeds $20$ (as $5^2$ is the only square $\ge 25$ dividing $2^{20}-1$).

\label{lem:local}
Fix a prime $q \ge 5$ and one of the four forms $f \in \{x,\, 6x-1,\, 2x-1,\, 6x-7\}$. The set of primes $p \equiv 7 \pmod{36}$ with $q^2 \mid f(x_p)$ (where $x_p = (p-1)/6$) is exactly the set of primes in a single residue class modulo $36q^2$ that is admissible (coprime to the modulus); by Dirichlet's theorem it has relative density
\[
\frac{1}{\varphi(q^2)} = \frac{1}{q(q-1)}
\]
within the class $p \equiv 7 \pmod{36}$. Moreover, for distinct forms these residue classes are distinct, so the corresponding events are mutually exclusive at the level of a single prime $q$.
